# Cline rules — demand-reorder-advisor

## Project
Single-agent (v1) demand forecasting + policy/inventory verification system for
a synthetic retail company. Full context: see docs/Problem_brief.md before
writing any code — it defines what's in scope and what's explicitly deferred.

## What Cline should do
- Write boilerplate: API clients, parsers, CRUD/DB glue, test scaffolding,
  FastAPI/MCP server plumbing, config loading.
- Follow the pattern notes in skills/ for the relevant area before writing new
  code in it — read the matching skills/*.md file first.

## What Cline should NOT do
- Do not write the agent's reasoning/prompt logic (system prompts, tool
  selection logic, critique logic) unsupervised — draft it, but every prompt
  gets reviewed line by line before it's kept.
- Do not add a new Python dependency without it being added to pyproject.toml
  in the same change.
- Do not invent business rules. Every number (thresholds, lead times, safety
  stock formula) must trace back to one of the 7 policy PDFs in
  data/policy_docs/ or docs/Problem_brief.md.

## Conventions
- Python 3.11+, type hints on every function signature.
- All structured LLM outputs are Pydantic models, validated via instructor —
  never parse raw JSON strings by hand.
- All LLM calls go through the single modular client in
  src/demand_advisor/llm_client.py (litellm-backed) — never call a provider
  SDK directly.
- Secrets only via .env / pydantic-settings — never hardcoded, never printed
  in logs.
- Every new tool function gets a test in tests/ in the same change.
- Every non-trivial design decision gets a short ADR in docs/adr/ using
  docs/adr/template.md — copy the template, don't write from scratch.
